Sparsey runs in three modes, learning, recogition, and recall. The latter two are two different kinds of retrieval. Retrieval is also referred to as inference. That is, when a model retrieves an answer, it can essentially be viewed as making an inference, i.e., delivering its best guess given an input query. In the case of recognition, the input query is some particular input, X, and the answer given by the model consists in activating the code (memory trace, internal representation) of X that will have been assigned at some earlier time, e.g., on a previous presentation, i.e., a learning trial, of X. But the query, or test, presentation of X, might in general not be exactly the same as X, but just similar to X. In the case of recall, the query is generally some portion of a previously presented input, e.g., the first few frames of a sequence, and the answer inference given by the model is the sequential activation of the remaining frames of the sequence in their correct order. 

In the current implementation, any given run of the model is in exactly one of these three modes. However, in the human brain, it seems that these modes can be switched between rapidly, and on-line.  For example, at one instant, a human might be functioning is something close to a pure retrieval (inference) mode, e.g., walking to work along the same path that has been walked a thousand other times. In that scenario, the sequence of sensory inputs that occurs might be quite similar, at some scale, to previously experienced sequences.  In Sparsey, that would correspond to the G (familiarity) measure being near 1 in many of the macs, particularly in the higher levels. But suppose that at the next instant, some wildly different event occurs, e.g., Santa's sleigh zips by right over his head. This is a highly novel, i.e., highly unfamiliar, event and would correspond to G near 0 in many macs.  In those macs, that is precisely the situation in which new learning (i.e., storage of novel codes representing the novel event) should occur.  That is, on any frame in which G is near 0 in a mac, that mac should essentially enter learning mode.  But then, just as rapidly, the situation might return to normal, i.e., become highly familiar again, i.e., G near 1 in most macs. As the human is again experiencing a familiar sequence of sensory inputs, he will essentially be in retrieval mode.  That is, when G near 1, the code activation dynamics are set to greatly favor reactivation of codes (memories) of inputs that are the most simmilar to the incomming inputs.

We intend to generalize Sparsey so that switching between modes can be done on-line, even from one frame to the next.  Furthermore, this flexibility should be implemented at the level of the individual mac. 
